WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.510
Mind Your Decisions. I'm Presh Talwalkar

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.890
Start with the trapezoid where these two sides are congruent

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.590
The trapezoid has angles of 80 degrees 80 degrees 100 degrees and 100 degrees

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:18.539
Now join congruent trapezoids to form a closed ring

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.789
How many trapezoids are needed in total to form a closed ring?

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:30.899
This is said to be one of the toughest questions asked on the SAT, which I saw on a thread on Quora

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:37.889
Pause if you'd like to give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:53.739
So, how can we solve this problem

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:57.009
We can actually simulate it on the computer quite nicely

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.739
We can actually place trapezoids adjacent to each other until we form a closed ring

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:10.030
We then just need to count the total number of trapezoids and there we go. We get our answer of 18

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:17.319
However, you won't be able to use computer software on the test. So how do we solve it using pencil and paper?

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:25.929
Well, let's place adjacent trapezoids and then calculate this exterior angle around the closed ring

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.570
Since we have an 80 degree angle here

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:35.679
This exterior angle will be 180 minus 80 minus 80 which simplifies to be 20 degrees

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:40.898
Now we'll use a property of exterior angles in a polygon

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:46.029
The sum of the exterior angles will be equal to 360 degrees

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.300
Since 1 exterior angle is 20 degrees

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:57.069
we have n times 20 degrees is equal to 360 and therefore n is equal to 18 and

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.240
That will be our answer which exactly matches what we found before

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:06.729
What's more is we can use this method in the video to solve similar problems?

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:13.660
so I challenge you to this variation of the problem where one angle and the trapezoid is 60 degrees and

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:18.190
I challenge you to another variation where one angle is 75 degrees

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:22.179
How many trapezoids will be needed to form a closed ring?

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:27.459
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.959
As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

